{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And she finished giving him to drink.</b> It should have said ״וַיְכַל לִשְׁתּוֹת״ (\"and he finished drinking\"). The intention is to say that she would bring the water close to him to drink until the very end of the drinking, and he did not need to exert himself to tilt the water toward himself." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that she was concerned for him on account of his being weary from the road, and that drinking too much would be harmful. She estimated in her mind an amount that would suffice for his drinking, and she did not wish to give him more lest he come to danger, for a weary person does not properly gauge his drinking at the time of his thirst. And so that he not think it was for the purpose of minimizing her effort that she intended this, she said to him <b>also for your camels… until if they have finished</b> — and I will not deal with them by the same estimation of sufficiency. From this you learn that it was out of compassion and love that she intended this for him, for the reason mentioned." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained according to the statement of the Sages, of blessed memory (Berakhot 40a), on the verse (Deuteronomy 11:15) ״וְנָתַתִּי עֵשֶׂב בְשָׂדְךָ לִבְהֶמְתְּךָ״ — that a person must first feed his animal and afterward himself. And you should know that this applies specifically where there is no danger or suffering involved; but where there is danger or suffering, one must first be concerned for his own suffering and afterward for his animal. Therefore, when the man said ״הַגְמִיאִינִי מְעַט״ (\"let me sip a little\"), the righteous woman sensed that he was thirsty for water and was suffering; therefore she said to him ״שְׁתֵה״ (\"drink\"). And once she had given him the amount she estimated, so that surely there was no longer any concern about the suffering of thirst — <b>and she finished giving him to drink, and said, also for your camels I will draw</b> — for in such a situation, where you are not suffering, it is proper to give precedence. And this is why Scripture juxtaposed to ״וַתְּכַל לְהַשְׁקֹתוֹ״ the phrase ״וַתֹּאמֶר גַּם לִגְמַלֶּיךָ״ and so on. And its saying <b>until if they have finished</b> means: I will not estimate for them according to conjecture, but rather I will draw and not stay my hand until I see water before them and they are no longer drinking — and this is your sign that they have finished drinking."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:09.810483631Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}